Wire Flutter's overscroll physics and indicators into GPUI's scroll containers (`div` with overflow scroll).

What:
- `gpui::overscroll::Overscroll` (`Bounce`, `Stretch`, `Glow`, `None`) and `GestureTuning::overscroll`, default
  `None` (GPUI's hard stop, so upstream behaviour is unchanged until a platform or app sets it). A container
  overrides it with `StatefulInteractiveElement::overscroll(..)`; `.overscroll(Overscroll::None)` opts it out.
- `overscroll/scroll_position.rs`: the glue, ported from Flutter's `ScrollPosition.setPixels`,
  `ScrollPositionWithSingleContext.applyUserOffset` / `goBallistic`, `ScrollDragController.end` / `cancel` and
  `BallisticScrollActivity` (`_tick`, `_end`, `applyMoveTo`): per axis, one `Activity` (`Idle`, `Drag`,
  `Ballistic`) and one `Indicator` (`Off`, `Stretch`, `Glow`), driven by touch scroll steps (`state × phase → next`
  in `handle_drag`) and frames (`tick`). Drags go through 0012's `apply_physics_to_user_offset` and
  `apply_boundary_conditions`; the release starts 0012's `create_ballistic_simulation` with the container's extents
  and the recognizer's release velocity, so a bouncing fling into an edge springs past it and back, and a clamping
  one hands its velocity to the stretch (`absorb_impact`). The refused overscroll feeds 0015's indicators.
- `div`: the scroll listener sends touch scrolls (`ScrollSource::Touch`) to it and ignores the recognizer's own
  momentum steps after the release (the container runs Flutter's simulation instead); wheels and trackpads stop a
  drag or fling and stay clamped, as Flutter's `PointerScrollEvent`. Prepaint ticks it, leaves an axis it is moving
  unclamped, requests frames while it animates, and moves each child as `StretchEffect`'s scale would move its
  centre (GPUI has no scale transform for every primitive, so sizes stay). Paint draws the glow per
  `GlowController::paint` (`_GlowingOverscrollIndicatorPainter`'s geometry, mirrored or transposed per edge) in
  Material 2's default secondary colour.
- `uniform_list` stays a hard stop: its visible range assumes an in-range offset.
- `TouchGestureRecognizer::fling_velocity`, `Window::gesture_tuning` / `touch_fling_velocity` (crate-private): the
  release velocity Flutter's `DragEndDetails` carries, read during the release's scroll step.
- Tests: `overscroll::scroll_position::tests::touch_overscroll_follows_flutter`, one row per behaviour, replaying
  touch traces (0013's `TraceEvent`, now crate-visible) through the test platform: bounce friction and spring back,
  a fling bouncing at the bottom, `None`, a per-container opt-out, stretch rising and receding, glow showing and
  fading, a wheel staying clamped, and nested containers each overscrolling only on their own axis.

Why: Parallax's platform behaviour table picks overscroll per target (bounce on iOS and macOS, stretch on
Android); GPUI's containers only clamp. The physics and indicators are already Flutter ports (0012, 0015), so this
patch is the glue Flutter's `ScrollPosition` is, ported the same way. It lives in `div.rs` because the scroll
offset, its clamp and the scroll listener are `Interactivity`'s private state; no extension trait can reach them.
At about 450 changed lines plus 380 test lines it is over the 200-line guide: the glow painter, the stretch and the
position state are one concern (what a container does past its edge) and none works without the others' glue.
